A||N||12||sdelgado||Fiero panel removal||||1||
Z||000000||sdelgado||05-03-2001||01:01 PM||||Oops!  I posted my question in the general chat room.  I guess here is my technical question!<BR>1. I am starting to remove the body panels of an 84 2m4 and I need help! Is there a process in doing this?  windshield firs, then the hood, trunk, fenders, etc???<P>2. Do I need any special tools for this?  I have a drill for the rivets and a wrench set, anything else?<P>Thanks,<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||84Bill||05-03-2001||01:07 PM||||Fenders, Roof, Rear upper, facias<BR>Roof is toughest. I found a link to aid you in the removal of that. You can remove the rear upper pannel  with the roof in place but the roof nuts need to be removed first and the rear of the roof pannel lifted about 1/2 inch or so.||||reg||3
Z||000002||sdelgado||05-03-2001||03:21 PM||||I guess I will leave the roof for last.  What about the bumpers, are they hard to remove? <P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Rodrv6||05-03-2001||04:18 PM||||The bumper facias are probably some of the easier panels to remove.  Just a few screws in the fender wells and under the side marker lights and some push fasteners for the rear.  The front has a few more bolts holding it on, but it's easy.  You can remove the roof panel without removing the windshield.  Peel the rubber trim from around the window, remove the bolts, and CAREFULLY cut through the adhesive goo that surrounds the sides and top of the windshield opening.  The sections of the roof that run down beside the windshield are very fragile and will crack easily, so be careful there.  When I took my skins off of my 84 the general sequence I used was:<BR>Hood and Decklid<BR>Rocker panels<BR>Front and Rear Facias<BR>Front Fenders<BR>Quarter panels (behind doors)<BR>Roof<BR>Rear Roof Section<BR>Door Skins <BR>Have fun  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||1
Z||000004||sdelgado||05-03-2001||06:40 PM||||well, I am getting started right now and I will follow the sequence you suggested.  Thanks!  <P>------------------<BR>||||reg||7
Z||000005||Wolfhound||05-03-2001||07:27 PM||||As for as tools, an 1/8 inch rod punch works great on the plastic rivits under the rocker pannels and under the bottom of the door panels. Knock the center in aand pry the rivit out. <BR>lift the center of the rear facia rivits carefully and you can reuse them.<BR>Coy||||reg||1
Z||000006||Richard Parnell||05-03-2001||07:30 PM||||I recently took my rocker panels off to replace the left front fender (among other things).  I drilled the rivets and plugs out.  What can I use to remount the panles solidly?||||reg||5
Z||000007||sdelgado||05-03-2001||07:37 PM||||I could of used the punch for the plastic rivets under the front fasia!  I just had a hard time pulling them with pliers and cutters.  I will use one for the rear, thanks.  <P>I will definately like to know what to use to put the bumpers back instead of those plastic rivets??<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Wolfhound||05-03-2001||08:37 PM||||Harbor Freight has a Large Capacity Riveter. $16.95 It comes with 25 1/4 inch rivets and three 25 packs of smaller ones.<BR>Item: #41291<BR>The 1/4 inch will work for the rocker panels. I,ve had no problem with the plastic rivits for the rear facia. If you break any you can find comparable rivets at Lowes Building Supply in the fastner section or most parts stores. <BR>sdelgato, pull out the center in the rear facia rivits, the punch only applys to the rocker and the rivets on the bottom edge of the door panel.||||reg||1
Z||000009||mbramble||05-03-2001||10:37 PM||||For the rocker panels I used collapsing metal hollow wall anchors.  The shortest ones I could find have a shank on them about 3/8".  When collapsed all the way down they clamp tightly to the metal.  It was a bear getting some of them to start collapsing without just spinning in the hole bit I was able to hold onto them with needlenosed pliers.  Now I simply use a screw and washer to hold the rocker panels, the lower quarter panels and the lower holes in the front fender in place.  Easy to remove and replace.||||reg||1
Z||000010||sdelgado||05-03-2001||11:05 PM||||thank you all for the info!  I ran out of light so I will continue tomorrow... so far so good...I manage to get the doors, front fenders, nose and hood and most of the all the interior.  As suggested, I will do the roof for last and take it easy.<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Wolfhound||05-04-2001||07:11 AM||||mbramble, I used hollow wall anchors on the first one I did.<BR>What worries me about them is since they are steel they will probably be very difficult to get out if I should have the need. Nothing like a spinning rivet or anchor with a carbon steel bit stuck in it to make your day.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||sdelgado||05-04-2001||12:58 PM||||Hmmm... I will consider that when is time to put some of the panels back in my project Fiero.  Thanks.<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||12
